\section{Ordinary return values}{

Character scalars become literal text and character vectors are joined with
newlines. Other JSON-serializable values become JSON in one text content
block. Bare lists are ordinary values, not MCP result objects. Ordinary
\code{NULL} becomes the text \code{"{}"}; an ordinary unnamed \code{list()} becomes \code{"[]"}.

For per-tool conversion choices, return your own JSON as ordinary character
text, for example \code{as.character(jsonlite::toJSON(value, na = "null"))}.
JSON-looking strings and jsonlite \code{json} objects are never parsed or inserted
as raw JSON. In \code{structured_content}, they remain string values.
}

\section{Structured-content mappings}{

Structured values follow \code{\link[jsonlite:toJSON]{jsonlite::toJSON()}} and the payload options below,
with one root convention: a bare empty list at the structured root represents
\code{{}}. Nested unnamed empty lists retain jsonlite's \verb{[]} mapping. \code{NULL}
means absence of the structured-content field, not a JSON null value.

With default options:\tabular{ll}{
   R value \tab Structured-content meaning \cr
   \code{list(ok = TRUE)} \tab JSON object \cr
   \code{c("a", "b")} \tab JSON array \cr
   \code{"done"} \tab JSON string scalar, not parsed as JSON \cr
   \code{list()} or \code{structure(list(), names = character())} \tab Empty object \code{{}} \cr
   \code{I(list())} \tab Explicit empty array \verb{[]} \cr
   \code{NA} or \code{NA_character_} \tab JSON \code{null} \cr
   \code{NA_integer_}, \code{NA_real_}, or \code{NA_complex_} \tab JSON string \code{"NA"} \cr
   \code{NULL} \tab No structured-content field \cr
}


Classes, dimensions, and explicit \code{I()} or \code{\link[jsonlite:unbox]{jsonlite::unbox()}}
representations retain their jsonlite mappings. Tool authors are
responsible for conformance with any advertised output schema.
}

\section{Payload JSON options}{

Configure these package-global R options before constructing results and
starting the server, and keep them stable while the server runs:
\itemize{
\item \code{mcplite.json.null}: \code{"null"} (default) or \code{"list"}. Named payload \code{NULL}
fields remain present, as JSON \code{null} or \code{{}}, respectively.
\item \code{mcplite.json.na}: unset by default, which omits jsonlite's \code{na} argument
and preserves its class-specific defaults. Set \code{"null"} or \code{"string"} to
select that jsonlite mapping. \code{"null"} also maps \code{NaN} and infinities to
JSON \code{null} and retains missing data-frame fields instead of omitting them.
\item \code{mcplite.json.auto_unbox}: \code{TRUE} (default) or \code{FALSE}. Setting \code{FALSE}
keeps singleton payload vectors as arrays; explicit \code{I()} and
\code{jsonlite::unbox()} representations still apply.
}

Remove an option with \code{NULL}, for example \code{options(mcplite.json.na = NULL)},
to restore its default. This omits \code{na}; it does not forward \code{na = NULL}.
These options apply to ordinary noncharacter return-value JSON and to
structured content, its representability checks, generated text fallback,
and protocol-version projection. Protocol fields, schemas, annotations,
and all result and content metadata retain fixed encoding.
}

